A||N||13||87fastback||MASSIVE vibration at 60+ MPH??||||1||
Z||000000||87fastback||11-07-2001||12:17 AM||||This is on an 87 V6 5-speed fastback.  The car drives fine in town and any speeds under about 50 mph.  But when you get to about 55 or over the car vibrates VERY bad.  So bad that you have to put two hands on the wheel to control it.  I have had the tires on the front rebalanced twice now and they are fine.  Checked the front suspension and it all is tight (put car on jackstands and checked it over).<P>It also pops very loudly when turning the wheel all the way to one side or the other and then releasing it.  Sounds like it is coming from the front suspension or rack.<P>Any suggestions would help.<P>ebay4now@yahoo.com<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||11-07-2001||08:46 AM||||Hey Ebay!<BR>The poping isn't normal, get it looked at...<BR>Also look the rear suspention over too...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick's Dad||11-07-2001||09:03 AM||||This sounds like ball joints and/or the steering mechanism (tie rods) to me.<P>Your description is somewhat familiar, and my 85 coupe had it's passenger front wheel fold into the wheel well (thankfully, in a parking lot, not on the highway) after a short time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierochic88||11-07-2001||07:51 PM||||I had a similiar problem and ended up having to replace two things:<P>1.  Steering Rack Bushing - but this only improved it about 50%.  Also:<BR>2.  I had gotten new tires and one actually had a faulty tread - new tires on car was much better!  I knew this because I tried on skiitimes gf's tires and it made a big difference, then I took my tires back and got new ones.<P>Jennifer<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||reddog||11-08-2001||08:49 PM||||I had a similar problem when I put my new wheels on. Went from stock to 17". Had the wheels balanced twice. But it still shook. Finally I took it to someone who specialized in aftermarket tuner type rims and they found that the problem was mainly because of some kind of bracket or shim on the hub that prevented the new wheels from being mounted fully-flush to the rotors. After they removed the "shims or brackets or whatever they where the car stopped shaking as bad. Balace was still off by a few oz. so they fixed that also. However I still have a small vibration in the steering at speeds over 80mph. but that is just because the rack has a small amount of play in it. Definitely have your steering and balljoints checked!! Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>   [ing]http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/on_fire_trail.gif[/img]<p>[This message has been edited by reddog (edited 11-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||87fastback||11-08-2001||08:58 PM||||Well, after searching I finally found the problem.  The rear is tight as a drum, but the front has ALOT of play in it.  After jacking the car up in the front and locking the steering I shook each wheel and there was alot of play in the rack where the tie-rod ends go in.  Mostly on the drivers side but both sides it was apparent.  Have new ones on order so hopefully this will end this problem.  Also looked to be the same source of the popping noise!<P>Thanks to all and I will let you all know the results.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||SLYGUY||11-09-2001||04:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by reddog:<BR><B> However I still have a small vibration in the steering at speeds over 80mph. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My car is doing the same thing. Took it to my local tire shop that I frequent and they told me that a wheel that is out of balance always vibrates between 40-60mph and that any vibration above that is most likely something with the suspension as other members have also said. The previous owner of my car thought it would be cool to lower just the front of the car by having another idiot put a welding torch to the front springs. I found this out when I had the brakes checked and saw that the bottom two coils of the front springs were touching. I really like the way it sits (not like some rally car as other GTs do) but not the way that it rides. Very firm. Sporty, but firm!<BR>I've decided to remedy this with a set of Eibach springs when the $ becomes available!!<P>Have a pro take a good look at your suspension. Good luck!!<P>Sly<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Primaris||11-09-2001||09:58 AM||||I am getting something very similar on my ’88 GT.  I get a rhythmic vibration at about 80-85 and it gets worse all the way up.  I have tried 2 sets of tires and balanced both sets 2 times.  The car has new ball joints on the front end and the tie rods are tight.   Now this vibration is not continuous.  It rises and falls.  In fact sometimes it will simply stop for a very short period of time.  I’m at a loss.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000008||87fastback||11-11-2001||12:41 PM||||Well, here we go again!  I changed the entire rack out to a new one, and though it helped some of the popping noise, it DID NOT help the vibration.  Plus there is still some popping there when I turn the wheel lock to lock and then back.  I am going to try the control arm bushings now as they look a little worn and there is some play in them.<P>Wish me luck!||||reg||8
Z||000009||theogre||11-11-2001||01:38 PM||||Primaris...<P>A vibration that comes and goes like that could also be a half shaft problem. The shaft could be bad or the bearings in the tranny could be sloppy.<P>Can also be bad engine mounts. Drivetrains can have some really bizare harmonics at times. A bad mount can cause/agravate such a problem.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Primaris||11-12-2001||01:15 PM||||Thanks for the info Orge.  I'm gonna check the engine mounts.  I was doing some 0 - 60 timing and I think I am getting way too much movement back there.  The *****  of the thing is I had a clutch put in a year or two ago and they charged me for new mounts.  It should be legal to kill any mechanic that lies or is incompetent.||||reg||1
Z||000011||samoht||11-12-2001||05:29 PM||||Jack the rear up and turn the wheel back and forth. you will feel a thump if one of the CV's are going..  check rims for chug ho dings. that what mine ended up being.. Rode great under 50..  I beat it out with a hammer.. just a little rib now .. as long as your wheel is GM it will ahve cut outs for those rotor retainers $.02||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||11-12-2001||06:37 PM||||My car seems to have a "moving" vibration above 60 or so mph. I first feel it in the steering wheel, then it moves to the seats, then looking in my mirror I can see the spoiler shake, then it starts in the front again. Any ides on this? It might be my tires, they are shot to **** , and I'm getting new ones tomarrow for my b-day, so maybe it will fix the problem. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly - 86 GT<BR>Back to my 3.1L...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||87fastback||11-15-2001||01:32 PM||||Well everyone will be happy to know I FINALLY found out what was making this vibration.  After going to the tire store to finally try new tires on the front, they went to pull the tire off the rim on the left side and upon doing so a bucket load of that fix-a-flat stuff came gushing out.  Looked to be to the tune of about 4 cans!  Seemed the previous owner decided it was easier to inflate a tire that way!<P>So after cleaning up the mess and rebalancing the tire, it is now as smooth as can be up to 80 mph, didn't want to push it anymore right now!<P>Thanks to all for all the insight, it was actually all the comments about the tire problems that lead me to this.  And remember when inflating tires, don't use fix-a-flat!<P>Thanks all!!||||reg||1
